<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samosata</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samosata was an ancient city on the right bank of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, at modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsat">Samsat</a> in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. It was the centre of the Neo-Hittite kingdom of Kummuh and later the capital of the Hellenistic kingdom of Commagene, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> annexed the region in 72 CE. The writer Lucian was born there, and the old site now lies under the reservoir of the Atatürk Dam.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samosata.jpg" alt="Samosata" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Radosław Botev · CC BY 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samsat_Turkey.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
